-- title:
--   Lebesgue measurability of the half-closed cube
-- statement:
--   For a real number $h$ and a centre $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let $\tilde A_h(u)$ (`halfClosedCube h u`) be the half-closed cube of side $h$ about $u$ — the cube taken closed on one side and open on the other in each coordinate, so that the family of such cubes over a lattice of centres tiles $\mathbb{R}^d$ exactly, every point lying in one and only one cube. The claim is that $\tilde A_h(u)$ is a measurable set.
--
--   Half-closed cubes are used in place of open or closed ones precisely so that the tiling is a genuine partition; measurability of each tile is what allows a function defined by its value on the tile containing a point to be handled measure-theoretically, and is a prerequisite for the associated averaging operators and Vitali-family arguments.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis whatever is placed on $h$: the statement holds for every real $h$, including $h \le 0$, where for $d \ge 1$ the coordinate condition is unsatisfiable and the cube is empty (in the degenerate case $d = 0$ the condition ranges over no coordinates at all, so the cube is the whole one-point space $\mathbb{R}^0$). The set is neither open nor closed in general, but it is a finite intersection of half-spaces and hence Borel.
-- source:
